{
  "count": 90,
  "cues": [
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-01",
      "start": 1.8,
      "end": 3.234,
      "text": "Snakes for hair."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-02",
      "start": 3.534,
      "end": 6.043333333333333,
      "text": "A gaze that turns people to stone."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-03",
      "start": 6.343333333333333,
      "end": 10.606666666666667,
      "text": "Medusa is one of the most\nfamous monsters of Greek myth."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-04",
      "start": 10.906666666666668,
      "end": 13.421701149425289,
      "text": "Today, she is also seen as a victim,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-04",
      "start": 13.421701149425289,
      "end": 16.984666666666666,
      "text": "a woman punished for something\nthat was done to her."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-05",
      "start": 17.284666666666666,
      "end": 18.77,
      "text": "So which is she?"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M01-06",
      "start": 19.07,
      "end": 21.920666666666666,
      "text": "The answer depends\non which source you read."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M02-01",
      "start": 23.933333333333334,
      "end": 28.949544973544974,
      "text": "In this episode, we follow Medusa\nacross seven centuries of Greek"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M02-01",
      "start": 28.949544973544974,
      "end": 34.514404761904764,
      "text": "and Roman poetry and art: an early\npoem about the gods, faces on ancient"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M02-01",
      "start": 34.514404761904764,
      "end": 39.92250793650794,
      "text": "shields and temples, a painted vase\nat The Metropolitan Museum of Art,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M02-01",
      "start": 39.92250793650794,
      "end": 43.68466666666667,
      "text": "and a Roman poet who told\nher story in a new way."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M03-01",
      "start": 49.1,
      "end": 55.43266666666667,
      "text": "One of the earliest accounts comes from\nthe Greek poet Hesiod, around 700 BCE."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M03-02",
      "start": 55.73266666666667,
      "end": 60.556517473118284,
      "text": "In his Theogony, a poem about\nthe origins of the gods, Medusa"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M03-02",
      "start": 60.556517473118284,
      "end": 65.70195833333334,
      "text": "is one of three Gorgon sisters,\ndaughters of ancient sea deities."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M03-03",
      "start": 66.00195833333333,
      "end": 69.59654166666667,
      "text": "Her sisters, Stheno\nand Euryale, are immortal."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M03-04",
      "start": 69.89654166666666,
      "end": 72.06316666666666,
      "text": "Medusa alone is mortal."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-01",
      "start": 72.96666666666667,
      "end": 75.61395833333333,
      "text": "Hesiod tells us very little about her."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-02",
      "start": 75.91395833333334,
      "end": 81.22420833333334,
      "text": "He says that Poseidon lay with her in\na soft meadow among spring flowers,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-02",
      "start": 81.22420833333334,
      "end": 85.87067708333333,
      "text": "and that when Perseus cut off\nher head, the winged horse Pegasus"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-02",
      "start": 85.87067708333333,
      "end": 90.074625,
      "text": "and Chrysaor, named for his golden\nsword, sprang from her."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-03",
      "start": 90.37462500000001,
      "end": 93.01058333333333,
      "text": "There is no curse and no punishment."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M04-04",
      "start": 93.31058333333334,
      "end": 96.90520833333333,
      "text": "In Hesiod, Medusa is simply born a Gorgon."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M05-01",
      "start": 98.73333333333333,
      "end": 102.93529166666667,
      "text": "Early Greek artists gave\nthe Gorgon an unforgettable face."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M05-02",
      "start": 103.23529166666667,
      "end": 107.52530166666666,
      "text": "In the sixth century BCE, she\noften looks like this: wide,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M05-02",
      "start": 107.52530166666666,
      "end": 112.64320833333333,
      "text": "staring eyes, a grinning mouth,\nsharp tusks, and a protruding tongue."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M05-03",
      "start": 112.94320833333333,
      "end": 115.23783333333333,
      "text": "She is not meant to be beautiful."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M05-04",
      "start": 115.53783333333334,
      "end": 117.363125,
      "text": "She is meant to be seen."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M06-01",
      "start": 118.73333333333333,
      "end": 122.95069548286604,
      "text": "Faces like this, called\ngorgoneia, appeared on shields,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M06-01",
      "start": 122.95069548286604,
      "end": 127.08995833333333,
      "text": "on drinking cups, and on the tiles\nalong temple roofs."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M06-02",
      "start": 127.38995833333334,
      "end": 130.99595833333333,
      "text": "Many scholars think they\nwere meant to frighten away harm."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M06-03",
      "start": 131.29595833333335,
      "end": 134.81395833333335,
      "text": "The monster's face could\nalso serve as a guardian."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M07-01",
      "start": 135.73333333333332,
      "end": 138.23,
      "text": "Even the goddess Athena carried it."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M07-02",
      "start": 138.52999999999997,
      "end": 141.8972544802867,
      "text": "As early as Homer's\nIliad, the Gorgon's head"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M07-02",
      "start": 141.8972544802867,
      "end": 145.81266666666664,
      "text": "appears on the aegis,\nAthena's protective goatskin."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M07-03",
      "start": 146.11266666666666,
      "end": 151.15666666666667,
      "text": "The face of the defeated monster\nbecame part of the goddess's own power."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M08-01",
      "start": 153.03333333333333,
      "end": 156.072,
      "text": "Over time, the image began to change."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M08-02",
      "start": 156.37199999999999,
      "end": 162.48266666666666,
      "text": "Around 490 BCE, the poet Pindar\nspoke of fair-cheeked Medusa."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M08-03",
      "start": 162.78266666666667,
      "end": 166.96569696969698,
      "text": "He also told how Athena\ninvented a melody for the aulos,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M08-03",
      "start": 166.96569696969698,
      "end": 171.98533333333333,
      "text": "a reed pipe, to imitate the lament\nof the Gorgons for their sister."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-01",
      "start": 172.9,
      "end": 177.19796642685853,
      "text": "On this vase\nat The Metropolitan Museum of Art,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-01",
      "start": 177.19796642685853,
      "end": 181.49593285371705,
      "text": "painted in Athens around\n450 BCE, Perseus comes"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-01",
      "start": 181.49593285371705,
      "end": 185.88733333333334,
      "text": "upon Medusa as she sleeps,\nwith Athena close by."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-02",
      "start": 186.18733333333333,
      "end": 190.334,
      "text": "Medusa still has wings, but\nher face is young and calm."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-03",
      "start": 190.63400000000001,
      "end": 193.96993023255814,
      "text": "The museum describes it\nas one of the earliest"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M09-03",
      "start": 193.96993023255814,
      "end": 197.00933333333333,
      "text": "images of Medusa with an attractive face."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M10-01",
      "start": 197.93333333333334,
      "end": 200.77395833333333,
      "text": "Why did artists begin\nto show her this way?"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M10-02",
      "start": 201.07395833333334,
      "end": 205.01916748366014,
      "text": "Scholars have suggested\nseveral reasons, from changing"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M10-02",
      "start": 205.01916748366014,
      "end": 208.666625,
      "text": "ideals of beauty to new\nways of telling the story."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M10-03",
      "start": 208.966625,
      "end": 211.81591666666668,
      "text": "The sources do not give a single answer."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M10-04",
      "start": 212.11591666666666,
      "end": 217.80991666666668,
      "text": "But by the fifth century BCE, Medusa\ncould be both terrifying and beautiful."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-01",
      "start": 219.63333333333333,
      "end": 223.22929166666665,
      "text": "The version best known today\ncomes later, from Rome."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-02",
      "start": 223.52929166666667,
      "end": 227.3911892361111,
      "text": "Around the year 8 CE,\nthe poet Ovid completed"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-02",
      "start": 227.3911892361111,
      "end": 231.95524999999998,
      "text": "his Metamorphoses, a long\npoem about transformations."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-03",
      "start": 232.25525,
      "end": 235.51991666666666,
      "text": "In Book Four, Perseus\nhimself tells the story."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-04",
      "start": 235.81991666666667,
      "end": 239.22156666666666,
      "text": "Medusa, he says, was once\na beautiful young"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M11-04",
      "start": 239.22156666666666,
      "end": 242.29925,
      "text": "woman, admired above all for her hair."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M12-01",
      "start": 243.2,
      "end": 247.40999545454545,
      "text": "Then, it is said, Neptune,\nthe Roman name for Poseidon,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M12-01",
      "start": 247.40999545454545,
      "end": 251.77591666666666,
      "text": "assaulted her in the temple\nof Minerva, the Roman Athena."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M12-02",
      "start": 252.07591666666664,
      "end": 255.01191666666665,
      "text": "The goddess hid her face behind her aegis."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M12-03",
      "start": 255.31191666666666,
      "end": 258.4185118312757,
      "text": "And so that the act\nwould not go unpunished,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M12-03",
      "start": 258.4185118312757,
      "end": 261.16387499999996,
      "text": "she changed Medusa's hair into snakes."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M13-01",
      "start": 262.06666666666666,
      "end": 265.6866048109966,
      "text": "Ovid wrote about seven\nhundred years after Hesiod,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M13-01",
      "start": 265.6866048109966,
      "end": 269.23266666666666,
      "text": "and he often reshaped\nolder myths in his own way."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M13-02",
      "start": 269.53266666666667,
      "end": 272.542625,
      "text": "Other ancient writers\ntell different stories."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M13-03",
      "start": 272.842625,
      "end": 278.3514148089172,
      "text": "The mythographer known as Apollodorus\nreports that, according to some, Medusa"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M13-03",
      "start": 278.3514148089172,
      "end": 284.222625,
      "text": "was beheaded for Athena's sake, because\nshe wished to rival the goddess in beauty."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-01",
      "start": 286.03333333333336,
      "end": 289.9466666666667,
      "text": "Medusa's story lived on long\nafter the ancient world."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-02",
      "start": 290.24666666666667,
      "end": 294.47158974358973,
      "text": "In the early 1800s,\nthe sculptor Antonio Canova"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-02",
      "start": 294.47158974358973,
      "end": 298.6046666666667,
      "text": "carved Perseus holding\nup her head in triumph."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-03",
      "start": 298.9046666666667,
      "end": 302.49833333333333,
      "text": "For Medusa's face, Canova\nfollowed an ancient"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-03",
      "start": 302.49833333333333,
      "end": 306.09200000000004,
      "text": "marble mask of a beautiful,\nsorrowful Medusa."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M14-04",
      "start": 306.39200000000005,
      "end": 309.18733333333336,
      "text": "The hero is the center of this statue."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M15-01",
      "start": 310.1,
      "end": 315.0963839506173,
      "text": "In the twentieth century, many\nwriters and artists returned to Ovid's"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M15-01",
      "start": 315.0963839506173,
      "end": 320.0192916666667,
      "text": "version and read Medusa as a woman\nwho was wronged, and then blamed."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M15-02",
      "start": 320.3192916666667,
      "end": 323.4132916666667,
      "text": "That reading is a modern interpretation."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M15-03",
      "start": 323.7132916666667,
      "end": 326.21125,
      "text": "But it is built on an ancient text."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M16-01",
      "start": 328.03333333333336,
      "end": 330.8939583333334,
      "text": "So is Medusa a monster or a victim?"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M16-02",
      "start": 331.19395833333334,
      "end": 333.97925000000004,
      "text": "In Hesiod, she is born a Gorgon."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M16-03",
      "start": 334.27925000000005,
      "end": 337.805875,
      "text": "On early temples and shields,\nher face protects."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M16-04",
      "start": 338.105875,
      "end": 342.2471666666667,
      "text": "On a classical vase, she\nsleeps with a young, calm face."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M16-05",
      "start": 342.54716666666667,
      "end": 346.621125,
      "text": "And in Ovid, she is a woman\npunished for what was done to her."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M17-01",
      "start": 349.06666666666666,
      "end": 351.90595833333333,
      "text": "There is no single original Medusa."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M17-02",
      "start": 352.20595833333334,
      "end": 355.31262499999997,
      "text": "Each source shows how\na different time imagined her."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M17-03",
      "start": 355.612625,
      "end": 357.92995833333333,
      "text": "Every story has an origin."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M17-04",
      "start": 358.22995833333334,
      "end": 359.992625,
      "text": "Medusa has many."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M18-01",
      "start": 363.56666666666666,
      "end": 368.396,
      "text": "This video is based on ancient\ntexts and public museum collections."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M18-02",
      "start": 368.69599999999997,
      "end": 371.65257142857143,
      "text": "The narration uses synthetic speech,"
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M18-02",
      "start": 371.65257142857143,
      "end": 375.5946666666667,
      "text": "and some images are\nAI-generated reconstructions."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M18-03",
      "start": 375.89466666666664,
      "end": 379.85066666666665,
      "text": "Sources and terms of use are\nlisted in the description."
    },
    {
      "sentence_id": "M18-04",
      "start": 380.15066666666667,
      "end": 381.92329166666667,
      "text": "Thank you for watching."
    }
  ],
  "timing": "Exact synthesized sentence boundaries; within-sentence pages weighted by text length",
  "script_matches_ignoring_layout_whitespace": true
}
